    "summary": "Complete exam-ready guide to Basic Concepts of Economics.",
    "body": "<div class=\"hero\">\n    <h1>Economics — Complete Notes</h1>\n    <div class=\"hero-sub\">Basic Concepts · National Income · Banking · Planning · Budget · Reforms</div>\n    <div class=\"hero-line\"></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"legend\">\n    <span class=\"legend-item legend-blue\">📘 Facts</span>\n    <span class=\"legend-item legend-red\">🔴 Key Terms</span>\n    <span class=\"legend-item legend-amber\">⚡ Exam</span>\n    <span class=\"legend-item legend-teal\">📌 Revision</span>\n    <span class=\"legend-item legend-green\">✓ Success</span>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"section-header\">SECTION 1 — Basic Concepts of Economics</div>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">1</span>What is Economics?</h2>\n\n  <p>Economics is the study of how people, businesses, and governments produce, distribute, and consume goods and services.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"infobox\">\n    <strong>Father of Economics:</strong> Adam Smith · Wrote <em>\"The Wealth of Nations\"</em> in 1776\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Two Branches</h3>\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Branch</th><th>Study Of</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Microeconomics</strong></td><td>Individual units (a consumer, a firm, a market)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Macroeconomics</strong></td><td>Whole economy (national income, inflation, unemployment)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fill-note\">✦ Economics is about how we use limited resources to satisfy unlimited wants ✦</div>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">2</span>Basic Economic Terms — Goods & Services</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Goods</strong></td><td>Tangible items</td><td>Food, clothes, cars</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Services</strong></td><td>Intangible activities</td><td>Banking, teaching</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Final Goods</strong></td><td>For final consumption</td><td>Bread, milk</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Intermediate Goods</strong></td><td>Used to make final goods</td><td>Flour, sugar</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Consumer Goods</strong></td><td>For direct use</td><td>Food, clothes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Capital Goods</strong></td><td>Used to produce other goods</td><td>Machinery, tools</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Public Goods</strong></td><td>Provided by government</td><td>Roads, defense</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Private Goods</strong></td><td>Provided by private firms</td><td>Cars, phones</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">3</span>Demand and Supply</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Demand</strong></td><td>Amount of a product people are willing and able to buy</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Supply</strong></td><td>Total amount of a product available to consumers</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Law of Demand</strong></td><td>Higher prices → Less demand</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Law of Supply</strong></td><td>Higher prices → More supply</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Market Equilibrium</strong></td><td>Market demand = Market supply</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">4</span>Inflation and Deflation</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Inflation</strong></td><td>Increase in general price level</td><td>Money loses value</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Deflation</strong></td><td>Decrease in general price level</td><td>Money gains value</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Stagflation</strong></td><td>High inflation + High unemployment</td><td>Worst situation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Hyperinflation</strong></td><td>Very high inflation</td><td>Money becomes worthless</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Disinflation</strong></td><td>Slowdown in inflation rate</td><td>Prices still rise but slower</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Reflation</strong></td><td>Deliberate increase in money supply</td><td>To counter deflation</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  \n</div>\n\n<!-- ============ PAGE 2 — INFLATION TYPES + UNEMPLOYMENT ============ -->\n\n\n  \n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">5</span>Types of Inflation</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Rate</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Creeping Inflation</strong></td><td>Slow — 3% to 5%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Walking Inflation</strong></td><td>Moderate — 5% to 10%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Running Inflation</strong></td><td>Fast — 10% to 20%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Galloping Inflation</strong></td><td>Very fast — 20% to 100%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Hyperinflation</strong></td><td>Extremely fast — 100%+</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"exam-focus\">\n    <strong>Also Remember:</strong> <strong>Demand-Pull Inflation</strong> — too much money chasing too few goods · <strong>Cost-Push Inflation</strong> — increase in cost of production\n  </div>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">6</span>Unemployment — Complete Data</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Frictional</strong></td><td>Between jobs</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Structural</strong></td><td>Due to change in technology</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Cyclical</strong></td><td>Due to recession</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Seasonal</strong></td><td>Due to season</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Disguised</strong></td><td>More people than needed</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Voluntary</strong></td><td>Not willing to work</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Involuntary</strong></td><td>Willing to work but no job</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Technological</strong></td><td>Due to automation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Educated</strong></td><td>Educated but no job</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">7</span>Poverty and Inequality</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Poverty Line</strong></td><td>Minimum income needed to meet basic needs</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>BPL</strong></td><td>Below Poverty Line</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>APL</strong></td><td>Above Poverty Line</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Gini Coefficient</strong></td><td>Measure of income inequality (0 = perfect equality, 1 = perfect inequality)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Lorenz Curve</strong></td><td>Graphical representation of income distribution</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>HDI</strong></td><td>Human Development Index — Health, Education, Income</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"infobox\">\n    <strong>HDI Measures 3 Things:</strong> Health (life expectancy) · Education (years of schooling) · Income (GNI per capita)\n  </div>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">8</span>Previous Year Questions</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"columns\">\n    <p><strong>Q1.</strong> What is GDP? <em>Total value of goods and services produced within a country in a year</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q2.</strong> What is GNP? <em>GDP + Net income from abroad</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q3.</strong> What is NNP? <em>GNP – Depreciation</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q4.</strong> What is Per Capita Income? <em>National Income ÷ Population</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q5.</strong> What is inflation? <em>Increase in general price level</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q6.</strong> What is deflation? <em>Decrease in general price level</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q7.</strong> What is stagflation? <em>High inflation + High unemployment</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q8.</strong> What is frictional unemployment? <em>Between jobs</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q9.</strong> What is disguised unemployment? <em>More people than needed</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q10.</strong> Full form of HDI? <em>Human Development Index</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q11.</strong> What is Demand? <em>Willingness and ability to buy</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q12.</strong> Market equilibrium? <em>Market demand = Market supply</em></p>\n  </div>\n\n  \n</div>\n\n<!-- ============ PAGE 3 — NATIONAL INCOME ============ -->"
