    "body": "<div class=\"section-header\">SECTION 3 — Banking in India (RBI)</div>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">14</span>What is Banking?</h2>\n\n  <p>A bank is a financial institution that accepts deposits and gives loans.</p>\n\n  <h3>Types of Banks</h3>\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Central Bank</strong></td><td>Reserve Bank of India (RBI)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Commercial Banks</strong></td><td>SBI, PNB, HDFC, ICICI</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Cooperative Banks</strong></td><td>Urban and Rural</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Regional Rural Banks (RRBs)</strong></td><td>For rural areas</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Development Banks</strong></td><td>IDBI, SIDBI, NABARD</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Specialized Banks</strong></td><td>EXIM Bank, SIDBI</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Payment Banks</strong></td><td>Paytm Payments Bank</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Small Finance Banks</strong></td><td>Ujjivan, Equitas</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">15</span>Reserve Bank of India (RBI)</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Established</strong></td><td><span class=\"critical\">1st April 1935</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Nationalized</strong></td><td><span class=\"critical\">1st January 1949</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Headquarters</strong></td><td>Mumbai</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>First Governor</strong></td><td>Sir Osborne Smith</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>First Indian Governor</strong></td><td>C.D. Deshmukh</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Current Governor (2025)</strong></td><td>Sanjay Malhotra</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Role</strong></td><td>Central Bank of India</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Functions</strong></td><td>Issue currency · Control credit · Manage forex · Banker's bank</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>RBI Act</strong></td><td>Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"exam-focus\">\n    <strong>Exam Focus:</strong> RBI was established on <strong>1st April 1935</strong> and nationalized on <strong>1st January 1949</strong>. It is called the <strong>\"Banker's Bank\"</strong> of India.\n  </div>\n\n  \n</div>\n\n<!-- ============ PAGE 5 — MONETARY POLICY ============ -->\n\n\n  \n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">16</span>Monetary Policy Tools</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"flowchart\">\n    <div class=\"flow-row\">\n      <div class=\"flow-box f-repo\">Repo Rate</div>\n      <div class=\"flow-box f-rev\">Reverse Repo</div>\n      <div class=\"flow-box f-crr\">CRR</div>\n      <div class=\"flow-box f-slr\">SLR</div>\n      <div class=\"flow-box f-om\">Open Market</div>\n      <div class=\"flow-box f-msf\">MSF</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Repo Rate</strong></td><td>Rate at which RBI lends to commercial banks</td><td>Increase → Loans become costly</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Reverse Repo Rate</strong></td><td>Rate at which RBI borrows from commercial banks</td><td>Increase → Banks park money with RBI</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>CRR</strong></td><td>Cash Reserve Ratio — % of deposits banks keep with RBI</td><td>Increase → Less money to lend</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>SLR</strong></td><td>Statutory Liquidity Ratio — % of deposits banks keep with themselves</td><td>Increase → Less money to lend</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Bank Rate</strong></td><td>Rate at which RBI lends long-term</td><td>Increase → Loans become costly</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Open Market Operations</strong></td><td>Buying and selling of government securities</td><td>Buy → More money in economy</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>MSF</strong></td><td>Marginal Standing Facility — Emergency borrowing</td><td>Higher than repo rate</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Simple Explanation</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Repo Rate:</strong> RBI lends to banks at this rate. If it increases, loans become expensive.</li>\n    <li><strong>Reverse Repo Rate:</strong> RBI borrows from banks. If it increases, banks park money with RBI.</li>\n    <li><strong>CRR:</strong> Banks must keep a percentage of deposits with RBI.</li>\n    <li><strong>SLR:</strong> Banks must keep a percentage of deposits with themselves.</li>\n    <li><strong>Bank Rate:</strong> Long-term lending rate.</li>\n    <li><strong>Open Market Operations:</strong> RBI buys/sells government securities to control money supply.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">17</span>Previous Year Questions</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"columns\">\n    <p><strong>Q1.</strong> When was RBI established? <em>1st April 1935</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q2.</strong> When was RBI nationalized? <em>1st January 1949</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q3.</strong> Where is RBI headquarters? <em>Mumbai</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q4.</strong> First Governor of RBI? <em>Sir Osborne Smith</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q5.</strong> First Indian Governor? <em>C.D. Deshmukh</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q6.</strong> What is Repo Rate? <em>Rate at which RBI lends to commercial banks</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q7.</strong> What is Reverse Repo Rate? <em>Rate at which RBI borrows from commercial banks</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q8.</strong> Full form of CRR? <em>Cash Reserve Ratio</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q9.</strong> Full form of SLR? <em>Statutory Liquidity Ratio</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q10.</strong> Full form of NABARD? <em>National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q11.</strong> Full form of SIDBI? <em>Small Industries Development Bank of India</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q12.</strong> Full form of EXIM Bank? <em>Export-Import Bank of India</em></p>\n  </div>\n\n  \n</div>\n\n<!-- ============ PAGE 6 — ECONOMIC PLANNING ============ -->"
