    "summary": "Complete exam-ready guide to Economic Reforms.",
    "body": "<div class=\"section-header\">SECTION 6 — Economic Reforms</div>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">23</span>What are Economic Reforms?</h2>\n\n  <p>Economic Reforms are changes made to the economic policies of a country to improve its performance. India introduced major economic reforms in 1991.</p>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">24</span>LPG Reforms (1991) — Complete Data</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Year</strong></td><td><span class=\"critical\">1991</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Prime Minister</strong></td><td>P.V. Narasimha Rao</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Finance Minister</strong></td><td>Dr. Manmohan Singh</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>LPG</strong></td><td>Liberalization, Privatization, Globalization</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Purpose</strong></td><td>To solve balance of payments crisis</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Result</strong></td><td>Economic growth, Foreign investment</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Simple Explanation</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Liberalization:</strong> Removing government restrictions on business.</li>\n    <li><strong>Privatization:</strong> Selling government-owned companies to private owners.</li>\n    <li><strong>Globalization:</strong> Integrating India's economy with the world economy.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div class=\"exam-focus\">\n    <strong>Exam Focus:</strong> The <strong>1991 LPG Reforms</strong> changed India's economy. They opened India to foreign investment and increased economic growth. Remember: <strong>P.V. Narasimha Rao</strong> (PM) and <strong>Dr. Manmohan Singh</strong> (FM).\n  </div>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">25</span>Previous Year Questions</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"columns\">\n    <p><strong>Q1.</strong> When were the LPG Reforms introduced? <em>1991</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q2.</strong> Who was the Prime Minister during LPG Reforms? <em>P.V. Narasimha Rao</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q3.</strong> Who was the Finance Minister during LPG Reforms? <em>Dr. Manmohan Singh</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q4.</strong> Full form of LPG? <em>Liberalization, Privatization, Globalization</em></p>\n    <p><strong>Q5.</strong> Why were LPG Reforms introduced? <em>To solve balance of payments crisis</em></p>\n  </div>\n\n  \n</div>\n\n<!-- ============ PAGE 9 — MASTER SUMMARY ============ -->\n\n\n  \n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">26</span>Master Summary — All Facts Combined</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"quick-rev\">\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Father of Economics:</strong> Adam Smith (1776)</li>\n      <li><strong>GDP:</strong> Gross Domestic Product · <strong>GNP:</strong> Gross National Product · <strong>NNP:</strong> Net National Product · <strong>NDP:</strong> Net Domestic Product</li>\n      <li><strong>National Income:</strong> NNP at Factor Cost · <strong>Per Capita Income:</strong> NI ÷ Population</li>\n      <li><strong>Demand:</strong> Willingness + ability to buy · <strong>Supply:</strong> Total available · <strong>Equilibrium:</strong> Demand = Supply</li>\n      <li><strong>Inflation:</strong> Price rise · <strong>Deflation:</strong> Price fall · <strong>Stagflation:</strong> High inflation + High unemployment</li>\n      <li><strong>Unemployment Types:</strong> Frictional · Structural · Cyclical · Seasonal · Disguised</li>\n      <li><strong>Poverty:</strong> Poverty Line · BPL · APL · Gini Coefficient (0 = equal, 1 = unequal) · HDI</li>\n      <li><strong>RBI Established:</strong> 1st April 1935 · <strong>Nationalized:</strong> 1st January 1949 · <strong>HQ:</strong> Mumbai</li>\n      <li><strong>First RBI Governor:</strong> Sir Osborne Smith · <strong>First Indian:</strong> C.D. Deshmukh</li>\n      <li><strong>Monetary Tools:</strong> Repo Rate · Reverse Repo · CRR · SLR · Bank Rate · OMO · MSF</li>\n      <li><strong>Financial Year:</strong> 1st April to 31st March</li>\n      <li><strong>Balanced Budget:</strong> Income = Expenditure · <strong>Surplus:</strong> Income &gt; Expenditure · <strong>Deficit:</strong> Income &lt; Expenditure</li>\n      <li><strong>Fiscal Deficit:</strong> Total Exp – Total Receipts (excl. borrowings) · <strong>Revenue Deficit:</strong> Rev Exp – Rev Receipts · <strong>Primary Deficit:</strong> FD – Interest</li>\n      <li><strong>1st Five Year Plan:</strong> 1951-56 (Agriculture) · <strong>2nd:</strong> 1956-61 (Industry) · <strong>3rd:</strong> 1961-66 (Self-reliance)</li>\n      <li><strong>NITI Aayog:</strong> Established 1st January 2015 · Replaced Planning Commission · Chairperson: PM</li>\n      <li><strong>GST Introduced:</strong> 1st July 2017 · <strong>Amendment:</strong> 101st Constitutional · <strong>Slabs:</strong> 5%, 12%, 18%, 28%</li>\n      <li><strong>LPG Reforms:</strong> 1991 · PM: P.V. Narasimha Rao · FM: Dr. Manmohan Singh</li>\n      <li><strong>IMF HQ:</strong> Washington D.C. · <strong>World Bank:</strong> Washington D.C. · <strong>WTO:</strong> Geneva · <strong>UN:</strong> New York</li>\n      <li><strong>WHO:</strong> Geneva · <strong>ILO:</strong> Geneva · <strong>UNESCO:</strong> Paris · <strong>UNICEF:</strong> New York · <strong>FAO:</strong> Rome</li>\n      <li><strong>OPEC:</strong> Vienna · <strong>SAARC:</strong> Kathmandu · <strong>ASEAN:</strong> Jakarta</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">27</span>Direct &amp; Indirect Tax</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Direct Tax</strong></td><td>Income Tax, Corporate Tax</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Indirect Tax</strong></td><td>GST, Customs Duty</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h2><span class=\"num\">28</span>GST (Goods and Services Tax)</h2>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Introduced</strong></td><td>1st July 2017</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Amendment</strong></td><td>101st Constitutional Amendment</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Council Chairperson</strong></td><td>Union Finance Minister</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Slabs</strong></td><td>5%, 12%, 18%, 28%</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <p class=\"source\">Source: Economics — Complete Notes (SSC CHSL Syllabus)</p>\n\n  \n</div>"
